## 2. Technical regulations: what changed

### 2.1 No DRS, active aero

| Topic | 2022-2025 | 2026 |
|---|---|---|
| Overtaking aid | DRS flap, only for car within 1s, in DRS zones | No DRS. Active aero on **front and rear wings**, available to all cars in designated zones |
| Aero modes | Fixed wings (rear flap only moves) | **Z-mode** (high downforce, cornering) and **X-mode** (low drag, straights) |
| Passing help | DRS | **Overtake / boost mode**: extra electrical deployment for a car close to the one ahead (about 1s) **(verify exact rules)** |

Telemetry implications:
- Look for the **X-mode to Z-mode transitions** in speed/throttle/brake traces.
- Straight-line speed is no longer a clean DRS-on vs DRS-off comparison. Mode usage, energy state and gap all interact.

### 2.2 Power units

- 1.6L V6 turbo hybrid stays
- **MGU-H removed**
- **MGU-K raised to about 350 kW** (from about 120 kW); electrical share of total power is close to 50% **(verify)**
- Energy recovered per lap is capped (about 8.5 MJ) **(verify)**
- **100% sustainable fuel**
- Result: energy management is a first-class performance factor. Drivers lift/coast earlier, harvest in braking zones, and may see the car "run out of electrical power" at the end of long straights (often called **clipping** or **super-clipping**).

Telemetry implications:
- Speed drop-offs at the end of straights while still at full throttle
- Different harvesting strategies per team in qualifying vs race
- Lift-and-coast patterns that did not exist in this form before

### 2.3 Chassis and tyres (approximate, verify)

| Item | Direction of change |
|---|---|
| Wheelbase | Shorter (about 3400 mm) |
| Width | Narrower (about 1900 mm) |
| Minimum weight | Lighter (about 768 kg, revised during development) |
| Floor / ground effect | Less ground-effect dependence, simpler floor |
| Downforce and drag | Both reduced vs 2025 |
| Tyres | Pirelli; narrower front and rear, 18-inch wheels retained |

### 2.4 Other season context

- Six sprint weekends were originally planned. Calendar changes later reduced the number of remaining sprints.
- Bahrain and Saudi Arabia (April) were **cancelled** because of the Middle East conflict, cutting the calendar from 24 to 22. Bahrain was later reinstated at **Sepang, Malaysia** (non-sprint) **(verify date and result)**.
- Madrid debuts as a new Spanish GP venue.

